    "q-83574": {
      "dom_id": "step-q83574",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
