    "q-158129": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158129",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
